      text: "By the time Maya reached the twelfth floor, she had already changed her mind about the job three times. She had spent the whole weekend preparing. She had read the job description so many times that she could recite it, rehearsed answers to every question she could imagine and even practised her handshake on her flatmate, who had described it as \"firm but friendly\". She had chosen a dark blue suit because the recruiter had mentioned that the company had a fairly formal dress code. What she had not prepared for was the panel. When the receptionist opened the door, Maya saw not one interviewer but four, sitting behind a long table. Nobody had warned her. For a moment her mind went completely blank. The first questions went reasonably well. She talked about her experience, explained why she had left her previous job and gave examples of times she had dealt with difficult clients. Then the head of the department, a quiet man who had not said a word until then, leaned forward. \"Tell us about a mistake you've made at work,\" he said. Maya froze. She had prepared a neat answer about her strengths and weaknesses, but not this. So she told the truth. She described a project she had managed two years earlier, how she had promised a client a deadline she knew was unrealistic, and how the whole team had worked through the weekend to fix the problem she had created. She explained what she had learned and what she did differently now. When she finished, there was a long silence. She was sure she had ruined everything. She left the building convinced that she had come across as careless and unprofessional, and she spent the evening complaining to her flatmate. Two days later the recruiter phoned. The panel had been impressed, he said, precisely because she had been honest. Most applicants had given perfect, polished answers that sounded rehearsed. Maya's answer had shown real self-awareness. They wanted to offer her the job, with a starting salary slightly higher than she had expected. She accepted on the spot.",
