      title: "Искусство проигрывать",
      text: "In Silicon Valley, failure is almost a badge of honour. Entrepreneurs talk openly about the companies that collapsed before they finally succeeded, and some investors even prefer founders who have already failed at least once. Elsewhere, however, attitudes are very different. In many cultures an unsuccessful business is seen as a personal disgrace, and people who fail are expected to disappear quietly. Psychologists argue that the way we respond to setbacks matters far more than the setbacks themselves. Research on resilience suggests that people who bounce back quickly are not necessarily more talented or luckier than others. What they share is a particular way of interpreting events. Instead of seeing failure as proof that they are not good enough, they see it as information: something did not work, so they need to try a different approach. Take the example of Lena Morozova, who opened a café in Riga at the age of twenty-six. Within a year it had closed. \"I had underestimated everything,\" she says. \"The rent, the competition, how exhausting it would be. For months I was convinced I was simply a failure.\" She spent the next two years working for other people, learning the business from the inside. Her second café, which opened in 2019, is now one of three, and she employs forty people. Lena does not romanticise the experience. \"Failing hurt. It was humiliating, and there was a lot of self-doubt. But every mistake I made the first time taught me something I couldn't have learned from a book. I learned the hard way, but I learned.\" Experts warn, though, that the popular message that failure is always good can be misleading. Repeated failure without reflection simply leads to burnout. The key is to analyse honestly what went wrong, to accept responsibility without punishing yourself, and then to act differently. Perseverance only pays off when it is combined with a willingness to change. Perhaps the most important lesson is that success is rarely effortless, even when it looks that way from the outside. Behind most overnight successes are years of trial and error that nobody saw. Recognising this does not make failing any less painful, but it might make it a little less frightening.",
