    text: "When Daniel Price handed in his notice at a London advertising agency in 2021, he assumed he would never walk through its doors again. He had been working there for six years, had been promoted twice and was, by his own admission, bored. A rival agency had offered him a bigger salary and a more impressive job title, and he accepted without hesitation. Eighteen months later, he was back. \"The new job looked great on paper,\" he says. \"But I had underestimated how much I valued the people I'd left behind. At my old agency I'd had a manager who trusted me. At the new place I was being micromanaged every single day.\" Daniel is what HR professionals call a boomerang employee: someone who leaves a company and later returns. Such returns used to be rare and were often seen as a sign of failure. Many firms had a strict policy of never rehiring people who had resigned, regarding them as disloyal. Today attitudes have changed dramatically. According to one recent survey, almost one in five people who changed jobs in the past few years has since gone back to a former employer. For companies, the advantages are obvious. A returning employee already understands the culture, knows the systems and needs much less training. They also bring back new skills and fresh ideas picked up elsewhere. \"Someone who has seen how a competitor works is incredibly valuable,\" explains Rachel Ong, an HR director at a technology firm. \"And when people come back by choice, it sends a powerful message to the rest of the staff that the grass isn't always greener.\" There are risks too. Colleagues who stayed loyal may feel resentful if a returner is rewarded with a higher salary than they receive. And the problems that made someone leave in the first place may not have disappeared. Ong advises managers to have an honest conversation before rehiring anyone. \"You need to ask: what has changed, for them and for us? If the answer is nothing, it probably won't work the second time either.\" As for Daniel, he has no regrets about leaving or about returning. \"I needed to go away to realise what I had,\" he says. \"Now I'm here because I want to be, not because I'm afraid to leave.\"",
